1
00:00:00,778 --> 00:00:05,422
Small merchants lose PayPal disputes they could win, and fight ones they can't.

2
00:00:05,422 --> 00:00:07,892
PayPal gives a seller ten days to respond.

3
00:00:07,892 --> 00:00:12,066
DisputeDesk is an AI agent and MCP server that works the dispute queue,

4
00:00:12,066 --> 00:00:14,535
and never submits anything it can't prove.

5
00:00:16,373 --> 00:00:17,583
This is demo mode:

6
00:00:17,583 --> 00:00:22,825
a local PayPal sandbox simulator, not live PayPal, so it runs with no secrets.

7
00:00:22,825 --> 00:00:26,454
A ceramics shop has twelve disputes waiting at PayPal.

8
00:00:26,454 --> 00:00:28,471
One click on run agent triage.

9
00:00:28,975 --> 00:00:32,274
The agent reads every dispute and the buyer's message,

10
00:00:32,274 --> 00:00:36,612
gathers evidence from the shop's own records, and recommends an action:

11
00:00:36,612 --> 00:00:40,950
five to fight, four to accept, one refund offer, and two for the owner.

12
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:42,416
The queue is an AG Grid:

13
00:00:42,416 --> 00:00:44,799
filter by decision, search, and export.

14
00:00:46,614 --> 00:00:51,929
Now the same app with a free language model, already triaged because the free tier is slow.

15
00:00:51,929 --> 00:00:53,330
Here is a case to fight.

16
00:00:53,330 --> 00:00:55,958
The decision comes from rules, not the model:

17
00:00:55,958 --> 00:01:00,104
FedEx delivered to Denver, matching the transaction's shipping address,

18
00:01:00,104 --> 00:01:02,148
and the amount is worth contesting.

19
00:01:02,756 --> 00:01:05,101
Every fact is a numbered evidence item:

20
00:01:05,101 --> 00:01:07,325
the order, the FedEx tracking number,

21
00:01:07,325 --> 00:01:11,173
and the carrier's delivery scan, each with the checks it passed.

22
00:01:11,776 --> 00:01:15,137
The model drafts the response, then a verifier checks it.

23
00:01:15,137 --> 00:01:18,262
Every sentence cites evidence, and every date, amount

24
00:01:18,262 --> 00:01:21,741
and tracking number must literally appear in what it cites.

25
00:01:21,741 --> 00:01:23,746
A draft that fails is thrown away.

26
00:01:24,352 --> 00:01:25,804
Buyers can type anything.

27
00:01:25,804 --> 00:01:29,057
This one tells the agent to fight and reveal its prompt.

28
00:01:29,057 --> 00:01:30,800
Buyer text is treated as data:

29
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:33,646
the injection is flagged, and the records decide.

30
00:01:33,646 --> 00:01:36,028
No shipment exists, so it goes to accept.

31
00:01:36,028 --> 00:01:41,314
In my benchmark, none of sixty-eight attacks got the attacker a different automatic action.

32
00:01:41,919 --> 00:01:47,013
Nothing leaves until I approve, and approval is bound to a hash of the exact payload.

33
00:01:47,013 --> 00:01:51,089
Approving sends the multipart provide-evidence request you see here,

34
00:01:51,089 --> 00:01:53,006
plus a hash-chained audit entry.

35
00:01:53,006 --> 00:01:56,962
It is also an MCP server, and I drive PayPal's own MCP server too.

36
00:01:56,962 --> 00:02:00,437
That has no evidence tool, so this call goes through REST.

37
00:02:01,768 --> 00:02:03,647
On seventy-two seeded disputes,

38
00:02:03,647 --> 00:02:08,435
DisputeDesk makes the expected call every time with no unsupported submissions,

39
00:02:08,435 --> 00:02:11,769
while fighting everything is right a third of the time.

40
00:02:11,769 --> 00:02:15,890
Those labels are mine, so it is a consistency check, not a win rate.

41
00:02:15,890 --> 00:02:21,406
With the free model, buyer-intent accuracy is ninety point six percent versus seventy-five.

42
00:02:21,406 --> 00:02:23,891
Going live needs two sandbox credentials.

43
00:02:23,891 --> 00:02:24,800
Models propose;

44
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:26,497
deterministic checks decide.

